<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-windows10.0.22621.0</TargetFramework>
    <TargetPlatformMinVersion>10.0.19041.0</TargetPlatformMinVersion>
    <RootNamespace>VoiceWink</RootNamespace>
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
    <Platforms>x64</Platforms>
    <RuntimeIdentifiers>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifiers>
    <UseWinUI>true</UseWinUI>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Version>1.97.392</Version>
    <AssemblyVersion>1.97.392.0</AssemblyVersion>
    <FileVersion>1.97.392.0</FileVersion>
    <Company>DV Studio</Company>
    <Authors>Dieter Verlaeckt</Authors>
    <Product>VoiceWink</Product>
    <Description>Voice-to-text for Windows — press a hotkey, speak, text appears at your cursor.</Description>
    <Copyright>Copyright © 2025-2026 Dieter Verlaeckt / DV Studio</Copyright>
    <!-- GPLv3 §6 source-availability disclosure surfaced in DLL metadata
         (Get-ItemProperty surfaces these as "Project URL" / "Source URL").
         Required by the per-package attribution clauses of the MIT/Apache-2.0
         dependencies and by GPLv3 §6 for the binary itself. Added 2026-05-23
         as part of the indie-launch compliance pass. -->
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://voicewink.app</PackageProjectUrl>
    <!-- RepositoryUrl is the published home: github.com/dvstudio-app/VoiceWink — a repo
         created FRESH and fed one allowlisted snapshot commit per release, tagged
         v{Version} by the public-snapshot publisher (mechanism decided 2026-08-08 —
         deliberately NOT a transfer or a mirror of development history). Until
         go-live it is private, so this URL 404s for
         anyone else — expected: no public-channel release exists before then, and
         tester-ring recipients obtain source via the EULA's request route. -->
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/dvstudio-app/VoiceWink</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <!-- GPL-3.0-only (NOT -or-later): the project and its VoiceInk upstream are "GPL v3",
         and a derivative cannot unilaterally add an "or later" grant. -->
    <PackageLicenseExpression>GPL-3.0-only</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <ApplicationIcon>Assets\appicon.ico</ApplicationIcon>
    <WindowsPackageType>None</WindowsPackageType>
    <WindowsSdkPackageVersion>10.0.22621.45</WindowsSdkPackageVersion>
    <PublishAot>false</PublishAot>

    <!-- Build-path neutrality (2026-09-15): the shipped DLL must not name the machine that built
         it. v1.87.382 embedded the hosted CI checkout path (the private development repository's
         directory, down to src\VoiceWink\) three times in the public installer. Three sites, three
         controls; BuildPathNeutralityTests pins each against the assembly the tests reference:
         1. The compiler records the PDB path (CodeView debug-directory entry) and every source
            document path. PathMap rewrites the project directory prefix to the conventional
            /_/ root — the same shape ContinuousIntegrationBuild produces, chosen over that switch
            because it needs no git metadata (the public snapshot builds from an archive) and so
            cannot warn on a clean clone. Project-scoped on purpose: the test project keeps real
            paths, its CallerFilePath-anchored rows depend on them.
         2. Sentry's source generator (Sentry.Compiler.Extensions) copies EVERY compiler-visible
            build property into a module initializer as string literals — ProjectDir, and
            CsWin32InputMetadataPaths under the building user's NuGet cache (C:\Users\<name>\...).
            The SDK only reads them for AOT/trimming detection, neither of which this app uses;
            SentryDisableSourceGenerator is Sentry's own opt-out.
         3. Sentry's WriteSentryAttributes target adds [AssemblyMetadata("Sentry.ProjectDirectory",
            $(ProjectDir))] for in-app frame detection by FILE PATH — inert here (no PDB ships, so
            frames carry no paths). StripSentryProjectDirectoryAttribute below removes it. -->
    <PathMap>$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\=/_/</PathMap>
    <SentryDisableSourceGenerator>true</SentryDisableSourceGenerator>

    <!-- Which README the installer bundles — see the Content item below. -->
    <BundledReadmeSource>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\scripts\public-snapshot\README.public.md</BundledReadmeSource>
    <BundledReadmeSource Condition="!Exists('$(BundledReadmeSource)')">$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\README.md</BundledReadmeSource>

    <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Velopack production entry-point invariant — these two properties
         form a single unit. Removing either re-enables a failure path the
         spike (`spike/velopack-day1`) caught and fixed on a clean VM.

         1. DISABLE_XAML_GENERATED_MAIN: suppresses the WinUI generator's
            static `Program.Main` so our custom `src/VoiceWink/Program.cs`
            is the sole entry point. Lets us run our Velopack lifecycle
            hooks (OnAfterInstall) + UpdateExeSelfHeal + Bootstrap.Try-
            Initialize BEFORE WinUI starts.

         2. WindowsAppSdkBootstrapInitialize=false: disables the WinAppSDK
            module initializer that would otherwise auto-fire at JIT-load
            time of `VoiceWink.dll`, BEFORE `Program.Main` runs. On a clean
            VM without `Microsoft.WindowsAppRuntime.1.6` installed, the
            auto-init's `MddBootstrapInitialize2` call would crash the
            process before our Velopack hook could install the runtime.
            We call `Bootstrap.TryInitialize` ourselves in `Program.Main`
            stage 3 after ensuring the runtime is installed.

         `WindowsAppSdkDeploymentManagerAutoInitialize` is intentionally
         NOT set here — it gates `DeploymentManager` auto-init, which only
         applies to packaged (`WindowsPackageType=MSIX`) apps. We are
         unpackaged, so the property is inert. CI's test workflow sets it
         defensively for hosted-runner stability but production does not
         need it as an invariant.
         See docs/plans/2026-05-05-2103-installer-signing-autoupdate-plan/
         50-decision.md override items 6 and 9.
         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);DISABLE_XAML_GENERATED_MAIN</DefineConstants>
    <WindowsAppSdkBootstrapInitialize>false</WindowsAppSdkBootstrapInitialize>

    <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         UPDATE_CHECK_ENABLED release gate (Plan 4G).

         Default false BY DESIGN — and it stays false. The committed default
         keeps dev/IDE/CI builds from ever checking for updates; signed release
         builds opt in per-build via `-p:UpdateCheckEnabled=true` (see
         installer/release-update.{ps1,sh}). Do NOT flip this committed default
         to true: UpdateServiceTests pins it false and CI would go red. (The
         prerequisites it once waited on are now met anyway — the privacy
         disclosure is the bundled active asset's §4 (privacy-v*.md), and the
         R2 CDN at updates.voicewink.app is live.)

         What `-p:UpdateCheckEnabled=true` does:
           1. Defines UPDATE_CHECK_ENABLED preprocessor symbol → IUpdate-
              Service.CheckForUpdatesAsync stops short-circuiting to null.
           2. Emits `update-check-enabled.marker` into the publish dir
              (see EmitUpdateCheckMarker target below).

         NOTE: the marker is emitted but DELIBERATELY not consumed. The
         intended marker-driven privacy-disclosure gate was NOT built (decided
         2026-06-02) — it would be redundant. What
         actually gates a signed release is (a) the DRAFT/placeholder scan in
         scripts/check-release-assets.ps1 and (b) the fact that the bundled
         Assets/Legal/privacy-v*.md IS the disclosure (its §4 documents the
         update-check egress). The marker is kept only as a cheap publish-
         output breadcrumb; do not rely on it enforcing anything.

         See docs/plans/2026-05-05-2103-installer-signing-autoupdate-plan/
         50-decision.md (v1 scope) + 30-claude-revised-plan.md §4G.
         ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <UpdateCheckEnabled>false</UpdateCheckEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(UpdateCheckEnabled)' == 'true'">
    <DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants);UPDATE_CHECK_ENABLED</DefineConstants>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Build-time update-feed config (channel, gated/ungated flag, and — for a gated tester
       ring only — the Cloudflare Access service-token credentials), compiled into UpdateFeedConfig.

       The non-secret CHANNEL comes from this property — the release script passes
       `-p:UpdateFeedChannel=<channel>`. The generator decides GATED vs PUBLIC from that name
       through scripts/lib/release-channel.ps1 (any '-stable' suffix is public; 2026-09-09): a tester
       ring bakes the SECRET credentials, read from a gitignored file
       (installer/.update-feed-credentials) and NEVER passed on the command line (which would leak
       them into process args / build logs); a public channel bakes NO credentials and never opens
       that file. Step 3b of the release script verifies the outcome against the published DLL.

       Dev builds leave the channel empty → UpdateFeedConfig.IsConfigured == false → the
       update-check path is inert, regardless of UPDATE_CHECK_ENABLED. The generated file lands in
       obj/ (gitignored), so credentials are never committed.
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <UpdateFeedChannel Condition="'$(UpdateFeedChannel)' == ''"></UpdateFeedChannel>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!-- Statically include the generated file so it is in @(Compile) from evaluation time — for
       BOTH the WinUI XAML markup pass (which compiles a temp assembly from all C# sources to
       resolve x:Bind) AND CoreCompile. The GenerateUpdateFeedConfig target below runs early
       enough to guarantee the file exists before any compile pass reads it. obj/ is excluded
       from the default compile glob, so this is the only Compile entry for it (no duplicate). -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="$(IntermediateOutputPath)UpdateFeedConfig.g.cs" />
    <Compile Include="$(IntermediateOutputPath)SentryConfig.g.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Generate the file BEFORE every compile pass. BeforeBuild covers the earliest point;
       MarkupCompilePass1 (the WinUI XAML pass) and CoreCompile are listed explicitly so the file
       exists for each (non-existent target names in BeforeTargets are harmlessly ignored). -->
  <Target Name="GenerateUpdateFeedConfig" BeforeTargets="BeforeBuild;BeforeCompile;MarkupCompilePass1;CoreCompile">
    <Exec Command="powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File &quot;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\scripts\generate-update-feed-config.ps1&quot; -Channel &quot;$(UpdateFeedChannel)&quot; -CredsFile &quot;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\installer\.update-feed-credentials&quot; -OutFile &quot;$(IntermediateOutputPath)UpdateFeedConfig.g.cs&quot;" />
  </Target>

  <!-- Build-time Sentry DSN. The SECRET DSN is read from a gitignored file
       (installer/.sentry-dsn) by the generator and is NEVER passed on the command line.
       EmbedSentryDsn is empty for dev/CI builds (DSN stays empty -> crash reporting inert,
       and SentryInitializerTests stays green); the signed release scripts pass
       -p:EmbedSentryDsn=true. Mirrors GenerateUpdateFeedConfig above. -->
  <Target Name="GenerateSentryConfig" BeforeTargets="BeforeBuild;BeforeCompile;MarkupCompilePass1;CoreCompile">
    <Exec Command="powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File &quot;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\scripts\generate-sentry-config.ps1&quot; -Embed &quot;$(EmbedSentryDsn)&quot; -DsnFile &quot;$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\installer\.sentry-dsn&quot; -OutFile &quot;$(IntermediateOutputPath)SentryConfig.g.cs&quot;" />
  </Target>

  <!-- Build-path neutrality site 3 (see the PathMap comment above). Sentry.targets'
       WriteSentryAttributes writes Sentry.Attributes.cs — one [AssemblyMetadata(
       "Sentry.ProjectDirectory", $(ProjectDir))] — and appends it to @(Compile) from the task's
       Output, so it cannot be pre-empted by property; it is removed from the compile set here,
       after that target has run and before CoreCompile reads the list. The file itself still
       lands in obj (harmless; FileWrites keeps it clean-able). -->
  <Target Name="StripSentryProjectDirectoryAttribute" AfterTargets="WriteSentryAttributes" BeforeTargets="CoreCompile">
    <ItemGroup>
      <Compile Remove="$(IntermediateOutputPath)$(SentryAttributesFile)" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'">
    <!-- WinUI XamlCompiler.exe fails in CLI Release builds when compiling the optimized intermediate assembly. -->
    <Optimize>false</Optimize>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="VoiceWink.Tests" />
    <!-- The dictation benchmark harness runs through the app's own internal pipeline pieces
         (WavPcm, the soft-speech gain trio, the HTTP policy table) - the standing rule in root
         CLAUDE.md that a harness must measure the SHIPPED path. tools/ only, never shipped. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="VoiceWink.AsrBench" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- WinUI 3 -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.WindowsAppSDK" Version="1.6.250602001" />

    <!-- MVVM -->
    <PackageReference Include="CommunityToolkit.Mvvm" Version="8.4.0" />

    <!-- Dependency Injection -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" Version="8.0.1" />

    <!-- HttpClient factory (retry handler + handler pool lifetime) -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Http" Version="8.0.1" />

    <!-- Database -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite" Version="8.0.11" />
    <!-- Direct pin: EF Core 8.0.x floors SQLitePCLRaw at 2.1.6, whose bundled SQLite
         is vulnerable (GHSA-2m69-gcr7-jv3q / CVE-2025-6965, fixed in SQLite 3.50.2).
         2.1.12 bundles SQLite 3.53.3. Do not remove as "redundant" — without it NuGet
         resolves the vulnerable transitive floor. -->
    <PackageReference Include="SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3" Version="2.1.12" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design" Version="8.0.11">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
      <IncludeAssets>runtime; build; native; contentfiles; analyzers; buildtransitive</IncludeAssets>
    </PackageReference>
    <PackageReference Include="Sentry.Serilog" Version="6.4.1" />

    <!-- Velopack — installer + auto-update mechanics. Pinned to 0.0.1298:
         the spike (`spike/velopack-day1`) validated this exact version end-to-end on
         a signed clean-VM install (UpdateManager + WaitExitThenApplyUpdates + delta
         updates + uninstall). The spike caught a Velopack 0.0.1298 signed-install bug
         where signed `vpk pack` (with the `azureTrustedSignFile` arg) sometimes fails
         to extract `Squirrel.exe` → `Update.exe` at the install root; the
         `Helpers/UpdateExeSelfHeal.cs` workaround re-extracts it on first launch.
         Do NOT unpin without re-running the full clean-VM signed matrix.
         See docs/plans/2026-05-05-2103-installer-signing-autoupdate-plan/50-decision.md
         override item 10. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Velopack" Version="0.0.1298" />

    <!-- Image processing (clipboard DIB conversion for image paste) -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Drawing.Common" Version="8.0.11" />

    <!-- Audio -->
    <PackageReference Include="NAudio" Version="2.2.1" />

    <!-- Whisper -->
    <PackageReference Include="Whisper.net" Version="1.9.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Whisper.net.Runtime" Version="1.9.1" />
    <!-- The GPU (Vulkan) whisper.cpp runtime, tried ahead of CPU when the
         pre-flight probe in Helpers/WhisperBackendLog proves the chain loadable (applied at
         App.ConfigureServices; a declined probe pins [Cpu] outright, because Whisper.net's
         own vulkan-to-cpu fall-through measurably aborts on a partial payload). Ships ~58 MB at
         runtimes\vulkan\win-x64\ — a SEPARATE directory from the CPU package's
         runtimes\win-x64\, and that separation is load-bearing: all four same-named DLLs
         (whisper / ggml-whisper / ggml-cpu-whisper / ggml-base-whisper) are DIFFERENT
         builds in the two packages, hash-verified 2026-09-01. This package's linux-x64
         ItemGroup carries NO Condition (59 MB of .so files a Windows app can never
         execute), so the StripVulkanLinuxNatives target below removes them; the publish
         gate (scripts/check-publish-payload.ps1) fail-closes if the strip regresses on a
         package bump. Do NOT add the CUDA package: the GPU-stack investigation
         (2026-09-01) measured it MOOT here — it
         needs the CUDA Toolkit runtime on the USER's machine and silently falls back to
         Vulkan (proven by loaded-module inspection). -->
    <PackageReference Include="Whisper.net.Runtime.Vulkan" Version="1.9.1" />

    <!-- sherpa-onnx: the SECOND native inference runtime. Pinned to the exact
         versions the CPU benchmark measured, so the numbers in
         docs/model-review/2026-08-02-parakeet-vs-local-whisper-cpu.md describe what ships.
         The runtime package is win-x64 only: the app targets that RID alone. -->
    <PackageReference Include="org.k2fsa.sherpa.onnx" Version="1.13.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="org.k2fsa.sherpa.onnx.runtime.win-x64" Version="1.13.4" />

    <!-- System Tray -->
    <PackageReference Include="H.NotifyIcon.WinUI" Version="2.1.0" />

    <!-- Global Hotkeys (Phase 5, but referenced in DI) -->
    <PackageReference Include="SharpHook" Version="5.3.8" />

    <!-- Window Extensions -->
    <PackageReference Include="WinUIEx" Version="2.5.0" />

    <!-- Logging -->
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog" Version="4.1.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.Sinks.File" Version="6.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Serilog.Sinks.Debug" Version="3.0.0" />

    <!-- DPAPI for API key encryption -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Security.Cryptography.ProtectedData" Version="8.0.0" />

    <!-- WMI for SMBIOS UUID read in hardware fingerprint -->
    <PackageReference Include="System.Management" Version="8.0.0" />

    <!-- P/Invoke Generation -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Windows.CsWin32" Version="0.3.106">
      <PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
    </PackageReference>
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Content Include="Assets\**" CopyToOutputDirectory="PreserveNewest" />
    <!-- GPL v3 §6: ship the LICENSE next to the binary so the EULA's source-availability
         claim is materially true, both in-app About/License surfacing and as a sibling
         file next to VoiceWink.dll in the bin output. The Link element forces it to land
         at the output root rather than under "..\..\". -->
    <Content Include="..\..\LICENSE">
      <Link>LICENSE</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <!-- The bundled README is the PUBLIC one (2026-09-15). The root README.md of the development
         repository is the contributor guide (build, test, CI, agent conventions) and shipped in every
         installer up to v1.87.382; the public snapshot publishes scripts/public-snapshot/
         README.public.md as its README.md instead. Bundle that file when it exists — every
         development and CI build — and fall back to the root README.md where it does not, which is
         exactly the public snapshot, whose root README.md IS that file (asserted byte-equal by the
         publisher). BundledReadmeTests pins the identity of the copy that reaches the output. -->
    <Content Include="$(BundledReadmeSource)">
      <Link>README.md</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <!-- The bundled Silero VAD model (Assets\Models) is MIT third-party content — its
         attribution must accompany the redistributed binary, so the notices file ships
         at the output root like LICENSE. Presence is enforced by the release payload
         and portable-ZIP gates. -->
    <Content Include="..\..\THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md">
      <Link>THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <!-- ONNX Runtime's OWN third-party notices. onnxruntime.dll statically
         links ~67 components (protobuf, Eigen, Abseil, ...) whose licences require their
         notices to travel with the binary, and neither the sherpa NuGet package nor our
         own notices file carries them. Kept as a separate file rather than inlined: it is
         325 KB and would bury everything else in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md.

         The version in the filename is read from the shipped DLL's ProductVersion, not
         from a build script. Presence is enforced by the release payload gate. -->
    <Content Include="..\..\licenses\onnxruntime-1.27.0-ThirdPartyNotices.txt">
      <Link>licenses\onnxruntime-1.27.0-ThirdPartyNotices.txt</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <!-- CHANGELOG.md ships next to the binary so the What's-new dialog can read it
         from AppContext.BaseDirectory. Same ..\..\ + Link shape as LICENSE / README. -->
    <Content Include="..\..\CHANGELOG.md">
      <Link>CHANGELOG.md</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>

    <!-- WindowsAppRuntimeInstall-x64.exe (~61 MB) — the bootstrap payload that
         Helpers/PrereqInstaller.cs spawns on clean machines without
         Microsoft.WindowsAppRuntime 1.6 pre-installed. Conditional include
         keeps the dev-loop fast: local Debug builds don't need the binary,
         and local Release builds emit a warning (not error) so iterative work
         isn't blocked on a 61 MB download.
         The fail-closed gate for the publish path is
         scripts/check-publish-payload.ps1 — it Authenticode-verifies the
         file is Microsoft-signed and within the expected size range, then
         hard-fails (exit 1) if anything is missing. /vw-deploy and the CI
         release workflow MUST run this script between `dotnet publish` and
         `vpk pack`. scripts/fetch-winapp-runtime.ps1 populates the file
         from Microsoft's CDN (SHA256-pinnable for CI). -->
    <Content Include="..\..\installer\runtime\WindowsAppRuntimeInstall-x64.exe"
             Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\installer\runtime\WindowsAppRuntimeInstall-x64.exe')">
      <Link>WindowsAppRuntimeInstall-x64.exe</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>

    <!-- App-local Microsoft Visual C++ runtime DLLs (~920 KB) — shipped next to
         whisper.dll at runtimes\win-x64\. The four native Whisper DLLs import the
         MSVC runtime (vcruntime140 / vcruntime140_1 / msvcp140, + vcomp140 for the
         CPU OpenMP backend); a clean Win 11 VM with no VC++ Redistributable fails
         the native load with PInvokeError 0. Velopack installs per-user/non-admin
         so vc_redist.x64.exe (machine-wide, admin-only) can't run — shipping these
         four app-local is the deterministic, Microsoft-permitted fix (they are on
         the VC++ "Redistributable Files" list; see installer/signing/README.md and
         docs/plans/2026-06-02-1939-vcredist-applocal-bundle/50-decision.md).

         Unlike WindowsAppRuntimeInstall-x64.exe (61 MB, gitignored + fetched per
         build), these are tiny and COMMITTED — every build/CI machine copies them
         with no extraction toolchain. scripts/fetch-vcredist.ps1 (WiX v5 + msiexec)
         is a maintainer-only refresh tool. The <Link> targets runtimes\win-x64\ so
         the DLLs land beside whisper.dll in both bin and publish output; the
         fail-closed gate scripts/check-vcredist-payload.ps1 proves it for the
         shipped artifact (Velopack nupkg + portable ZIP). Condition="Exists(...)"
         mirrors the WindowsAppRuntime block above. -->
    <Content Include="..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\vcruntime140.dll"
             Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\vcruntime140.dll')">
      <Link>runtimes\win-x64\vcruntime140.dll</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <Content Include="..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\vcruntime140_1.dll"
             Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\vcruntime140_1.dll')">
      <Link>runtimes\win-x64\vcruntime140_1.dll</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <Content Include="..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\msvcp140.dll"
             Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\msvcp140.dll')">
      <Link>runtimes\win-x64\msvcp140.dll</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <Content Include="..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\vcomp140.dll"
             Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\vcomp140.dll')">
      <Link>runtimes\win-x64\vcomp140.dll</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>

    <!-- The Khronos Vulkan LOADER, beside parakeet-server.exe in runtimes\win-x64\ — and
         DELIBERATELY nowhere else. The server statically imports vulkan-1.dll (first
         import-table entry, delay-load directory empty), so on a machine with no GPU driver
         (no system loader) the child would die before main, every spawn would fail, and the
         storm fuse would mark Parakeet — the default engine — unrunnable. With this copy in
         the CHILD's own directory the import resolves there first; a real driver's ICD still
         enumerates through it, and no driver at all means zero devices and a clean CPU
         fallback (both measured, two GPU vendors). The APP process never resolves this copy:
         its own vulkan-1 probe (WhisperBackendLog) searches the app dir + System32, so
         Whisper's GPU-less behaviour stays exactly as measured — do not "help" Whisper by
         copying the loader to the app root; a devices-less loader would flip its probe onto
         an unmeasured path. Pinned by installer/runtime/vulkan-loader/MANIFEST.psd1 and
         verified by check-vcredist-payload.ps1; attribution ships via THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md
         (Apache-2.0, gate-asserted). -->
    <Content Include="..\..\installer\runtime\vulkan-loader\vulkan-1.dll"
             Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\installer\runtime\vulkan-loader\vulkan-1.dll')">
      <Link>runtimes\win-x64\vulkan-1.dll</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>

    <!-- The SAME four VC++ DLLs again, beside the Vulkan whisper.dll at
         runtimes\vulkan\win-x64\. Load-bearing, not belt-and-braces: the [Vulkan, Cpu]
         pin makes the VULKAN copy the first whisper.dll the process loads, and Win32
         resolves a loaded DLL's imports from ITS OWN directory first — so on a clean VM
         (Velopack per-user install, no machine-wide VC++ Redistributable: exactly the
         population the vcredist bundle exists for) a Vulkan directory without these four
         fails the native load with PInvokeError 0, the loader falls through to CPU, and
         GPU acceleration silently never engages for the users it shipped for.
         scripts/check-vcredist-payload.ps1 verifies this directory with the same
         SHA-256/PE/signer checks as the CPU one. -->
    <Content Include="..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\vcruntime140.dll"
             Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\vcruntime140.dll')">
      <Link>runtimes\vulkan\win-x64\vcruntime140.dll</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <Content Include="..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\vcruntime140_1.dll"
             Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\vcruntime140_1.dll')">
      <Link>runtimes\vulkan\win-x64\vcruntime140_1.dll</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <Content Include="..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\msvcp140.dll"
             Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\msvcp140.dll')">
      <Link>runtimes\vulkan\win-x64\msvcp140.dll</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
    <Content Include="..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\vcomp140.dll"
             Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\vcomp140.dll')">
      <Link>runtimes\vulkan\win-x64\vcomp140.dll</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>

    <!-- parakeet-server.exe: the resident local-transcription helper the parakeet.cpp
         backend spawns - LOAD-BEARING in every ordinary build since the rollout flip
         (PcppEnabled defaults true; scripts/check-vcredist-payload.ps1 step 4 fail-closes
         the publish if this row's copy did not fire, because this Condition is fail-SOFT
         and a missing exe would silently ship the old engine forever). COMMITTED like the
         vcredist set (1.7 MB, deterministic, SHA-256 pinned in ParakeetBackendCoordinator
         and re-verified per LAUNCH before any spawn). The <Link> placement inside
         runtimes\win-x64\ is LOAD-BEARING, not cosmetic: the exe's PE import table needs
         msvcp140/vcruntime140/vcruntime140_1/vcomp140, those DLLs land in
         runtimes\win-x64\ (the rows above), and a SPAWNED child resolves imports from ITS
         OWN directory - the .NET native-probing path means nothing to the Win32 loader.
         Beside the DLLs, the closure holds on a clean machine by construction. -->
    <Content Include="..\..\installer\runtime\parakeet\parakeet-server.exe"
             Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\installer\runtime\parakeet\parakeet-server.exe')">
      <Link>runtimes\win-x64\parakeet-server.exe</Link>
      <CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
    </Content>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Local-build warning. The hard gate is scripts/check-publish-payload.ps1,
       invoked between `dotnet publish` and `vpk pack`. Errors here would
       block local Release builds on fresh clones without a fetch run first,
       so we keep this as a warning and let the publish-side script fail
       closed. -->
  <Target Name="WarnIfRuntimeInstallerMissing"
          BeforeTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release' AND !Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\installer\runtime\WindowsAppRuntimeInstall-x64.exe')">
    <Warning Text="WindowsAppRuntimeInstall-x64.exe missing from installer/runtime/. Run scripts/fetch-winapp-runtime.ps1 before publishing — scripts/check-publish-payload.ps1 will hard-fail the publish path without it." />
  </Target>

  <!-- App-local VC++ runtime DLLs are COMMITTED (installer/runtime/vcredist/), so
       this should never fire on a clean clone. It guards the case where someone
       deletes/relocates them — the publish would otherwise ship without the MSVC
       runtime and crash native Whisper load on a clean VM. The hard gate is
       scripts/check-vcredist-payload.ps1 (run by check-publish-payload.ps1 + the
       portable build); this is just an early local-build breadcrumb. -->
  <Target Name="WarnIfVcRedistMissing"
          BeforeTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release' AND !Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\..\installer\runtime\vcredist\vcruntime140.dll')">
    <Warning Text="App-local VC++ runtime DLLs missing from installer/runtime/vcredist/. They are normally committed; if absent, run scripts/fetch-vcredist.ps1 to regenerate them — scripts/check-vcredist-payload.ps1 will hard-fail the publish/portable path without them." />
  </Target>

  <!-- Whisper.net.Runtime.Vulkan 1.9.1's linux-x64 ItemGroup carries NO Condition
       (its Windows one is TFM-gated), so 59 MB of Linux .so files that cannot execute on
       Windows would ride every build AND publish. They arrive as None items with
       CopyToOutputDirectory — not RID-specific NuGet native assets — so a RID-specific
       publish does NOT prune them; this target does, before AssignTargetPaths hands them
       to the copy pipeline (which both build and publish output flow through). Matched on
       the items' own TargetPath metadata so a package relayout on a future bump fails
       toward SHIPPING the files — which scripts/check-publish-payload.ps1's fail-closed
       layout assertion then catches — never toward deleting someone else's payload. -->
  <Target Name="StripVulkanLinuxNatives" BeforeTargets="AssignTargetPaths">
    <ItemGroup>
      <None Remove="@(None)"
            Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(None.TargetPath)').StartsWith('runtimes/vulkan/linux-')) Or $([System.String]::Copy('%(None.TargetPath)').StartsWith('runtimes\vulkan\linux-'))" />
    </ItemGroup>
  </Target>

  <!-- Emits `update-check-enabled.marker` into the publish output when
       UpdateCheckEnabled=true. Content = the package Version so an auditor
       can see which build activated the gate. NOTE: no script consumes this
       marker — the marker-driven privacy-disclosure gate was deliberately NOT
       built (decided 2026-06-02): it would be redundant.
       What blocks an unreviewed signed release is the DRAFT scan in
       scripts/check-release-assets.ps1 plus the bundled Assets/Legal/privacy-v*.md
       being the disclosure itself (§4). The marker is kept only as a publish-
       output breadcrumb. See the UpdateCheckEnabled PropertyGroup above. -->
  <Target Name="EmitUpdateCheckMarker"
          AfterTargets="Publish"
          Condition="'$(UpdateCheckEnabled)' == 'true'">
    <WriteLinesToFile File="$(PublishDir)update-check-enabled.marker"
                      Lines="$(Version)"
                      Overwrite="true" />
  </Target>

  <!-- Emits `vad-disabled.marker` into the publish output when the VAD-disable response
       lever is pulled (-p:VadEnabled=false). Content = the package Version.

       UNLIKE update-check-enabled.marker above (a breadcrumb nothing consumes), this
       marker IS consumed: it is the runbook's static pre-ship check, letting an
       operator confirm a signed pack really is the VAD-disabled build without
       installing it and dictating. The runtime proof is the
       "VAD disabled by build" log line; this is the artifact-level proof.

       See the VadEnabled PropertyGroup in the root Directory.Build.props, which
       states when the lever is legitimate to pull and how to verify the build. -->
  <Target Name="EmitVadDisabledMarker"
          AfterTargets="Publish"
          Condition="'$(VadEnabled)' == 'false'">
    <WriteLinesToFile File="$(PublishDir)vad-disabled.marker"
                      Lines="$(Version)"
                      Overwrite="true" />
  </Target>

  <!-- The same artifact-level proof for the Parakeet lever
       (-p:ParakeetEnabled=false), and consumed the same way — the runbook's static
       pre-ship check, so an operator can confirm a signed pack really is the
       Parakeet-disabled build without installing it.

       Needed even though a disabled build's payload is byte-identical apart from one
       compiled-out branch: the sherpa natives still SHIP either way — both release gates
       require them (check-publish-payload.ps1 and check-release-assets.ps1), exactly as the
       bundled Silero model still ships in a VAD-lever build — so "are the DLLs present"
       cannot answer which build this is.

       See the ParakeetEnabled PropertyGroup in the root Directory.Build.props, which
       states when the lever is legitimate to pull and how to verify the build. -->
  <Target Name="EmitParakeetDisabledMarker"
          AfterTargets="Publish"
          Condition="'$(ParakeetEnabled)' == 'false'">
    <WriteLinesToFile File="$(PublishDir)parakeet-disabled.marker"
                      Lines="$(Version)"
                      Overwrite="true" />
  </Target>
</Project>
